<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<worksheet xml:id="Math234Fa24-FinalExam" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
	<title>Math 234 - Final Exam - Fall 2024</title>

	<exercise points="7">
		<introduction>
			<p>
				Consider the curve <m>\vec{r}(t) = \langle 2t+1, \sin(\pi t), t^2 - 2 \rangle.</m>
			</p>
		</introduction>
		<task points="4">
			<statement>
				<p>
					Write down an integral that represents the length of the curve from the point <m>(1, 0, -2)</m> to the point <m>(5, 0, 2).</m> You do NOT need to compute the integral.
				</p>
			</statement>
			<solution>
				<p>
					The point <m>(1, 0, -2)</m> corresponds to <m>t = 0</m>. The point <m>(5, 0, 2)</m> corresponds to <m>t = 2</m>.
					The derivative is <m>\vec{r}'(t) = \langle 2, \pi \cos(\pi t), 2t \rangle</m>.
					The speed is <m>|\vec{r}'(t)| = \sqrt{2^2 + (\pi \cos(\pi t))^2 + (2t)^2} = \sqrt{4 + \pi^2 \cos^2(\pi t) + 4t^2}</m>.
					The arc length is:
					<me>L = \int_0^2 \sqrt{4 + \pi^2 \cos^2(\pi t) + 4t^2} \, dt.</me>
				</p>
			</solution>
		</task>
		<task points="3">
			<statement>
				<p>
					Find an equation for the tangent line to the curve at the point <m>(5, 0, 2).</m>
				</p>
			</statement>
			<solution>
				<p>
					The point <m>(5, 0, 2)</m> occurs at <m>t = 2</m>.
					The direction vector of the tangent line is <m>\vec{r}'(2) = \langle 2, \pi \cos(2\pi), 4 \rangle = \langle 2, \pi, 4 \rangle</m>.
					The equation for the tangent line is:
					<me>\vec{L}(t) = \langle 5, 0, 2 \rangle + t \langle 2, \pi, 4 \rangle.</me>
				</p>
			</solution>
		</task>
	</exercise>

	<exercise points="12">
		<introduction>
			<p>
				Write <me>\displaystyle\iiint_{E} (x + 2z) \, dV</me> as an integral in the specified coordinate system. Here, <m>E</m> represents the solid that lies above the cone <m>z = \sqrt{x^2+y^2}</m> and below the sphere <m>x^2+y^2+z^2 = 9,</m> with <m>x &gt; 0.</m> You do NOT need to evaluate the integrals.
			</p>
		</introduction>
		<task points="6">
			<statement>
				<p>
					Cylindrical Coordinates
				</p>
			</statement>
			<solution>
				<p>
					In Cylindrical coordinates, <m>x = r \cos \theta, y = r \sin \theta, z = z</m>.
					The projection onto the xy-plane where <m>x &gt; 0</m> means <m>-\pi/2 \le \theta \le \pi/2</m>.
					Intersection of cone and sphere: <m>z^2 = r^2</m> and <m>r^2 + z^2 = 9 \implies 2r^2 = 9 \implies r = \sqrt{9/2}</m>.
					The integral is:
					<me>
						\int_{-\pi/2}^{\pi/2} \int_0^{\sqrt{9/2}} \int_r^{\sqrt{9-r^2}} (r \cos \theta + 2z) \cdot r \, dz \, dr \, d\theta.
					</me>
				</p>
			</solution>
		</task>
		<task points="6">
			<statement>
				<p>
					Spherical Coordinates
				</p>
			</statement>
			<solution>
				<p>
					In Spherical coordinates, <m>x = \rho \sin \phi \cos \theta, z = \rho \cos \phi</m>.
					The sphere <m>\rho^2 = 9</m> is <m>\rho = 3</m>.
					The cone <m>z = r \implies \rho \cos \phi = \rho \sin \phi \implies \tan \phi = 1 \implies \phi = \pi/4</m>.
					The region <m>x &gt; 0</m> means <m>-\pi/2 \le \theta \le \pi/2</m>.
					The integral is:
					<me>
						\int_{-\pi/2}^{\pi/2} \int_0^{\pi/4} \int_0^3 (\rho \sin \phi \cos \theta + 2 \rho \cos \phi) \rho^2 \sin \phi \, d\rho \, d\phi \, d\theta.
					</me>
				</p>
			</solution>
		</task>
	</exercise>

	<exercise points="11">
		<statement>
			<p>
				Compute <m>\displaystyle\iint_{\mathcal{S}} \text{curl}(\vec{F}) \cdot d\vec{S},</m> where <me>\vec{F}(x,y,z) = \langle -y+2z -4, xe^{z-2}, \ln(z^2+4) \rangle</me> and <m>\mathcal{S}</m> is the portion of the sphere <m>x^2+y^2+(z-2)^2 = 4</m> that lies below the plane <m>z=2,</m> oriented inward (that is, the normal vector at the origin points in the direction of the positive <m>z</m>-axis). If you use a theorem, clearly state which theorem you are using.
			</p>
		</statement>
		<solution>
			<p>
				Use Stoke's Theorem: <m>\iint_{\mathcal{S}} \text{curl}(\vec{F}) \cdot d\vec{S} = \oint_{\mathcal{C}} \vec{F} \cdot d\vec{r}</m>.
				The boundary curve <m>\mathcal{C}</m> is the circle where the sphere intersects <m>z=2</m>.
				<me>x^2 + y^2 + (2-2)^2 = 4 \implies x^2 + y^2 = 4.</me>
				Parametrize <m>\mathcal{C}</m>: <m>\vec{r}(t) = \langle 2\cos t, 2\sin t, 2 \rangle</m> for <m>0 \le t \le 2\pi</m>.
				Derivative: <m>\vec{r}'(t) = \langle -2\sin t, 2\cos t, 0 \rangle</m>.
				Evaluate <m>\vec{F}</m> on the curve:
				<me>\vec{F}(\vec{r}(t)) = \langle -2\sin t + 4 - 4, 2\cos t e^0, \ln(4+4) \rangle = \langle -2\sin t, 2\cos t, \ln 8 \rangle.</me>
				The integral is:
				<me>
					\int_0^{2\pi} \langle -2\sin t, 2\cos t, \ln 8 \rangle \cdot \langle -2\sin t, 2\cos t, 0 \rangle \, dt = \int_0^{2\pi} (4\sin^2 t + 4\cos^2 t) \, dt
				</me>
				<me>= \int_0^{2\pi} 4 \, dt = 8\pi.</me>
			</p>
		</solution>
	</exercise>

	<exercise points="11">
		<statement>
			<p>
				Rewrite the double integral <me>\displaystyle\int_0^2\int_0^{2-x}\sqrt{y-3x}(x+y)^8\, dy \, dx</me> as a double integral in <m>u</m> and <m>v</m> over an appropriate region by applying the transformation <m>u = x+y</m> and <m>v = y-3x</m>. You do NOT need to evaluate the integral.
			</p>
		</statement>
		<solution>
			<p>
				Solve for <m>x, y</m>: <m>u - v = 4x \implies x = \frac{u-v}{4}</m>.
				<m>y = u - x = u - \frac{u-v}{4} = \frac{3u+v}{4}</m>.
				Compute Jacobian: <m>J = \begin{vmatrix} 1/4 \amp -1/4 \\ 3/4 \amp 1/4 \end{vmatrix} = 1/16 - (-3/16) = 4/16 = 1/4 \implies |J| = 1/4</m>.
				Bounds in xy-plane: <m>x=0 \implies v=u, \ y=0 \implies v=-3u, \ y=2-x \implies u=2</m>.
				Region in uv-plane: <m>0 \le u \le 2</m> and <m>-3u \le v \le u</m>.
				The integral is:
				<me>
					\int_0^2 \int_{-3u}^u \sqrt{v} u^8 \cdot \frac{1}{4} \, dv \, du.
				</me>
				(Note: If changing order to <m>du \, dv</m>, the region splits at <m>v=0</m> as shown in the images).
			</p>
		</solution>
	</exercise>

	<exercise points="12">
		<introduction>
			<p>
				Consider the surface that is the part of the paraboloid <m>y=x^2+z^2</m> that lies within the cylinder <m>x^2+z^2=4.</m>
			</p>
		</introduction>
		<task points="4">
			<statement>
				<p>
					Write down a parametrization for the surface. Make sure to include bounds for your parameters.
				</p>
			</statement>
			<solution>
				<p>
					Using polar parameters for the disk in the xz-plane: <m>x = r \cos \theta, z = r \sin \theta</m>.
					The paraboloid is <m>y = x^2 + z^2 = r^2</m>.
					<me>\vec{r}(r, \theta) = \langle r \cos \theta, r^2, r \sin \theta \rangle \text{ for } 0 \le r \le 2, 0 \le \theta \le 2\pi.</me>
				</p>
			</solution>
		</task>
		<task points="8">
			<statement>
				<p>
					Find the surface area of the surface.
				</p>
			</statement>
			<solution>
				<p>
					Compute normal vector:
					<me>\vec{r}_r = \langle \cos \theta, 2r, \sin \theta \rangle, \quad \vec{r}_\theta = \langle -r \sin \theta, 0, r \cos \theta \rangle.</me>
					<me>\vec{r}_r \times \vec{r}_\theta = \langle 2r^2 \cos \theta, -r, 2r^2 \sin \theta \rangle.</me>
					Magnitude: <m>|\vec{r}_r \times \vec{r}_\theta| = \sqrt{(2r^2)^2 \cos^2 \theta + (-r)^2 + (2r^2)^2 \sin^2 \theta} = \sqrt{4r^4 + r^2} = r\sqrt{4r^2+1}</m>.
					Area is:
					<me>\int_0^{2\pi} \int_0^2 r\sqrt{4r^2+1} \, dr \, d\theta = 2\pi \int_0^2 r\sqrt{4r^2+1} \, dr.</me>
					Let <m>u = 4r^2+1, du = 8r \, dr</m>.
					<me>= 2\pi \int_1^{17} \frac{1}{8} \sqrt{u} \, du = \left. \frac{\pi}{4} \cdot \frac{2}{3} u^{3/2} \right|_1^{17} = \frac{\pi}{6}(17^{3/2} - 1).</me>
				</p>
			</solution>
		</task>
	</exercise>

	<exercise points="21">
		<introduction>
			<p>
				Compute the following integrals.
			</p>
		</introduction>
		<task points="7">
			<statement>
				<p>
					<m>\displaystyle\iint_{\mathcal{D}} e^{x^2+y^2}\, dA</m> where the region <m>\mathcal{D}</m> is between the curves <m>x^2+y^2=4</m> and <m>x^2+y^2 = 16,</m> below the line <m>y=x</m>, and below the line <m>y=0</m>.
				</p>
			</statement>
			<solution>
				<p>
					Use polar coordinates: <m>r^2 = x^2+y^2</m>, <m>dA = r \, dr \, d\theta</m>.
					Radius: <m>2 \le r \le 4</m>.
					Angle: Below <m>y=x</m> (<m>\pi/4</m>) and <m>y=0</m> (<m>0</m> or <m>2\pi</m>). The region in the fourth/third quadrant is <m>5\pi/4 \le \theta \le 2\pi</m>.
					<me>\int_{5\pi/4}^{2\pi} \int_2^4 e^{r^2} \cdot r \, dr \, d\theta = \frac{1}{2}(2\pi - 5\pi/4) [e^{r^2}]_2^4 = \frac{3\pi}{8}(e^{16} - e^4).</me>
				</p>
			</solution>
		</task>
		<task points="7">
			<statement>
				<p>
					<m>\displaystyle\int_0^1 \int_{\sqrt{y}}^1 \sqrt{x^3+1} \, dx \, dy</m>
				</p>
			</statement>
			<solution>
				<p>
					Change order of integration. Limits: <m>\sqrt{y} \le x \le 1, 0 \le y \le 1</m>.
					This is equivalent to <m>0 \le y \le x^2, 0 \le x \le 1</m>.
					<me>\int_0^1 \int_0^{x^2} \sqrt{x^3+1} \, dy \, dx = \int_0^1 x^2 \sqrt{x^3+1} \, dx.</me>
					Let <m>u = x^3+1, du = 3x^2 \, dx</m>.
					<me>= \int_1^2 \frac{1}{3} \sqrt{u} \, du = \left. \frac{1}{3} \frac{2}{3} u^{3/2} \right|_1^2 = \frac{2}{9}(2^{3/2} - 1).</me>
				</p>
			</solution>
		</task>
		<task points="7">
			<statement>
				<p>
					<m>\displaystyle\int_{\mathcal{C}} xy^2 \, ds,</m> where the curve <m>\mathcal{C}</m> is the portion of the circle of radius 3 centered at the origin in the first quadrant traversed counterclockwise.
				</p>
			</statement>
			<solution>
				<p>
					Parametrize <m>\mathcal{C}</m>: <m>\vec{r}(t) = \langle 3\cos t, 3\sin t \rangle</m> for <m>0 \le t \le \pi/2</m>.
					<m>ds = |\vec{r}'(t)| dt = 3 \, dt</m>.
					<me>\int_0^{\pi/2} (3\cos t)(9\sin^2 t) \cdot 3 \, dt = 81 \int_0^{\pi/2} \sin^2 t \cos t \, dt.</me>
					Let <m>u = \sin t, du = \cos t \, dt</m>.
					<me>= 81 \int_0^1 u^2 \, du = 81 [u^3/3]_0^1 = 27.</me>
				</p>
			</solution>
		</task>
	</exercise>

	<exercise points="10">
		<introduction>
			<p>
				Consider the vector field <m>\vec{F}(x,y) = \langle 2xy+ye^{xy} , x^2+xe^{xy}+2y \rangle.</m>
			</p>
		</introduction>
		<task points="2">
			<statement>
				<p>
					Show that <m>\vec{F}</m> is conservative.
				</p>
			</statement>
			<solution>
				<p>
					Check <m>P_y = Q_x</m>:
					<m>P_y = \frac{\partial}{\partial y}(2xy + ye^{xy}) = 2x + e^{xy} + xye^{xy}</m>.
					<m>Q_x = \frac{\partial}{\partial x}(x^2 + xe^{xy} + 2y) = 2x + e^{xy} + xye^{xy}</m>.
					Since <m>P_y = Q_x</m>, <m>\vec{F}</m> is conservative.
				</p>
			</solution>
		</task>
		<task points="5">
			<statement>
				<p>
					Find a potential function for <m>\vec{F}.</m>
				</p>
			</statement>
			<solution>
				<p>
					Integrate <m>f_x = 2xy + ye^{xy}</m> with respect to <m>x</m>:
					<m>f(x, y) = x^2y + e^{xy} + g(y)</m>.
					Differentiate with respect to <m>y</m>:
					<m>f_y = x^2 + xe^{xy} + g'(y)</m>.
					Setting <m>f_y = Q</m>: <m>x^2 + xe^{xy} + g'(y) = x^2 + xe^{xy} + 2y \implies g'(y) = 2y \implies g(y) = y^2 + C</m>.
					<me>f(x, y) = x^2y + e^{xy} + y^2 + C.</me>
				</p>
			</solution>
		</task>
		<task points="3">
			<statement>
				<p>
					Evaluate the integral <m>\displaystyle\int_{\mathcal{C}} \vec{F} \cdot d\vec{r},</m> where <m>\mathcal{C}</m> is parametrized by <me>\vec{r}(t) = \langle t\ln(5-t), t^2-3t \rangle,</me> with <m>0 \le t \le 4.</m>
				</p>
			</statement>
			<solution>
				<p>
					Use Fundamental Theorem of Line Integrals: <m>\int_{\mathcal{C}} \vec{F} \cdot d\vec{r} = f(\vec{r}(4)) - f(\vec{r}(0))</m>.
					Endpoints: <m>\vec{r}(4) = \langle 4\ln(1), 16-12 \rangle = \langle 0, 4 \rangle</m>.
					<m>\vec{r}(0) = \langle 0, 0 \rangle</m>.
					Integral is <m>f(0, 4) - f(0, 0) = (0 + e^0 + 16) - (0 + e^0 + 0) = 17 - 1 = 16.</m>
				</p>
			</solution>
		</task>
	</exercise>

	<exercise points="8">
		<statement>
			<p>
				Compute <m>\displaystyle\iint_{\mathcal{S}} \vec{F} \cdot d\vec{S},</m> for the vector field <me>\vec{F}(x,y,z) = \langle \ln(y^2+z^2+1) + 2x, y - e^{z^2 x}, \sin(xy) + x^2 y^3 \rangle,</me> outward through the surface <m>\mathcal{S}</m> of the box <me>E = \{ (x,y,z) \mid -2 \le x \le 1, -1 \le y \le 0, 0 \le z \le 4 \}.</me> If you use a theorem, clearly state which theorem you are using.
			</p>
		</statement>
		<solution>
			<p>
				Use the Divergence Theorem: <m>\iint_{\mathcal{S}} \vec{F} \cdot d\vec{S} = \iiint_E \text{div}(\vec{F}) \, dV</m>.
				Compute divergence: <m>\text{div}(\vec{F}) = 2 + 1 + 0 = 3</m>.
				<me>\iiint_E 3 \, dV = 3 \cdot \text{Volume}(E) = 3 \cdot (1 - (-2)) \cdot (0 - (-1)) \cdot (4 - 0) = 3 \cdot 3 \cdot 1 \cdot 4 = 36.</me>
			</p>
		</solution>
	</exercise>

	<exercise points="8">
		<statement>
			<p>
				Compute <me>\oint_{\mathcal{C}} \left( e^{x^3}\sqrt{x} + 2y \right) dx + \left( -3x + \sin(y^2) \right) dy</me> where <m>\mathcal{C}</m> is given by <m>\vec{r}(t) = \langle 2\cos t, 2\sin t \rangle</m> with <m>0 \le t \le 2\pi</m>. If you use a theorem, clearly state which theorem you are using.
			</p>
		</statement>
		<solution>
			<p>
				Use Green's Theorem: <m>\oint_{\mathcal{C}} P \, dx + Q \, dy = \iint_D (Q_x - P_y) \, dA</m>.
				<m>P = e^{x^3}\sqrt{x} + 2y \implies P_y = 2</m>.
				<m>Q = -3x + \sin(y^2) \implies Q_x = -3</m>.
				The integral is:
				<me>\iint_D (-3 - 2) \, dA = -5 \cdot \text{Area}(\text{Circle}) = -5 \cdot \pi(2)^2 = -20\pi.</me>
			</p>
		</solution>
	</exercise>
</worksheet>

